    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm trying to set up some reorder logic for my shop. we sell small electronics components and i'm worried about running out of lithium battery cells during our peak season. can you give me a basic formula or some rules to help me figure out how much safety stock i should keep on hand to avoid stockouts?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A basic formula for safety stock is to calculate your expected demand during the lead time, and then add a buffer based on the variability of that demand and your desired service level. You can use a formula involving the standard deviation of demand and your desired service factor (Z-score). For a simple start, try keeping enough stock to cover your average daily usage multiplied by your supplier's lead time, and then add a percentage buffer, perhaps 20-30%, as a starting point. This buffer is your initial \"safety stock\" to prevent those sudden, unexpected rushes.",
